/// MISCOMPILE-CLASS DECLINE — 2026-07-15, seed rcdzc (wasm backend), found by the `v-cad` vertical while
/// building the G1 `Solid` CAD model library (a real recursion/sums/records/floats stress test, GH #400).
///
///   error: "comparison of a compound value needs a heap walk (not yet built)"
///
/// `cdz check` is CLEAN; `cdz compile` DECLINES. The compared operands are SCALAR (`Float64`), so this is a
/// MISCLASSIFICATION: a *scalar* `==` is being routed to the *compound* (heap-walk) comparison path.
///
/// SHARP repro (this file, single-export so `cdz compile FILE -o -` reproduces): the trigger is REUSING a
/// COMPOUND-typed local binding MORE THAN ONCE. `vx(v)` and `vy(v)` each `match` the SAME `v : Vec3`; the
/// later `x == 1.0` (a Float64 compare) is then misrouted as a compound comparison and declines.
///
/// SHARP — each is NECESSARY (removing any one makes it compile + run correctly):
///   • the binding `v` must be COMPOUND-typed (a sum/record); a scalar `v` never trips it.
///   • `v` must be USED ≥ 2 TIMES. Using it ONCE (`let a = vx(v) in a == 1.0`) COMPILES + runs (control
///     `mlrepro-...-CONTROL`-style below). A SINGLE destructuring `match v with V3(x, y, _) => x==1.0 && …`
///     also COMPILES — so the workaround is "destructure once, don't re-project."
///   • it is independent of `==`'s side: even with NO `==` on the projections at all
///     (`let s = vx(v) + vy(v) in if s > 0.5 …`) it still declines — so the reused compound binding, not
///     the equality per se, is what corrupts the later comparison lowering.
///   • it is NOT the accessor helpers: inlining the two `match`es on the same `v` inline declines
///     identically; factoring the double-use INTO a helper (`def add2(v) = vx(v)+vy(v)`) and comparing only
///     `add2(...)`'s result COMPILES — so it's the compound binding being live across ≥2 projections in the
///     SAME scope, at the point a sibling scalar comparison is lowered.
///   • a TUPLE reused via `.0`/`.1` (`let p=(1.0,2.0) in p.0==1.0 && p.1==2.0`) COMPILES — so it is specific
///     to a SUM/record binding reused via `match` projection, not all compounds.
///   • it is FLOAT-SPECIFIC. The identical shape with `Int64` fields + an `Int64` compare
///     (`Box.B(1,2)`, `bx(v)==1 && by(v)==2`) COMPILES + runs. Only the Float64 comparison
///     (`==`, `<`, `>` — all reproduce) after a reused compound binding declines. So the misclassification
///     is in the FLOAT comparison lowering path specifically (fcmp), not the integer one.
///
/// LIKELY LOCUS: backend/wasm — the `value-eq` / comparison lowering (select.rs) decides scalar-vs-compound
/// from a liveness/ownership fact about nearby compound bindings rather than from the compared operand's OWN
/// type. When the compound binding stays live (a second projection ahead), the sibling scalar `=`'s lowering
/// reads the compound as the comparison operand. Reject-don't-miscompile fires (good — no wrong value), but
/// the classification is wrong: the operands ARE scalar.
///
/// IMPACT: pervasive for idiomatic data code — ANY function that reads two+ fields of the same value and
/// compares one is affected (a Vec3's x/y/z, a record's two fields). The CAD G1 library works around it by
/// destructuring each value with ONE `match` binding all components, never re-projecting the same binding.
///
/// `run` is exported (single export) so `cdz compile FILE -o -` reproduces the decline directly.
type Vec3 = | V3(Float64, Float64, Float64)

def vx(v: Vec3) = match v with | Vec3.V3(x, _, _) => x
def vy(v: Vec3) = match v with | Vec3.V3(_, y, _) => y

/// `v` is used TWICE (vx and vy). `vx(v) == 1.0` is a SCALAR Float64 compare but declines as compound.
/// Correct value would be 7.
def run() =
  let v = Vec3.V3(1.0, 2.0, 3.0) in
  if vx(v) == 1.0 then (if vy(v) == 2.0 then 7 else 8) else 9

export { run }

/// ⚠ OPEN but DO-NOT-RE-MINT-YET (2026-07-16): fix agents for this STALLED at startup (environmental issue, 6 stall instances this session — escalated to concierge/fleet-tooling as the #1 fix-pipeline blocker). Re-minting is confirmed futile until window.sh/startup is fixed. Well-characterized here; pick up once the env is repaired.
///
/// ═══ CORRECTED DIAGNOSIS (v-wasm-opt, 2026-07-16 — the filed root cause above is WRONG) ═══
/// The "reused compound binding / nearby-liveness misclassification in select.rs value-eq" story is a RED
/// HERRING that burned 5 fix-agents. DECISIVE test: a BARE two-param compare with ZERO compound bindings —
///     def run(x: Float64, y: Float64) = if x < y then 1 else 0
/// ALSO DECLINES with the same error. And `def run(x: Float64) = if x == 1.0 …` DECLINES. Meanwhile the
/// "single-use" control COMPILES only because `vx(constantVec3)` CONSTANT-FOLDS the whole compare away —
/// double-use just defeats const-prop, leaving a genuine RUNTIME float compare that then declines. So ALL
/// runtime float compares decline uniformly; the reused-binding/recursion "discriminators" are const-fold
/// artifacts, not the root.
/// TRUE ROOT: runtime float comparison is UNIMPLEMENTED. `is_scalar` (lower.rs:20160) is Int|Bool ONLY
/// (excludes Float), so `Core::Compare` dispatch (lower.rs ~16008) sends every runtime Float =/</>/<=/>= to
/// the compound-heap-walk `else` decline (16066). No float-compare machinery exists: Prim has FAdd/FSub/
/// FMul/FDiv only; Lir has F64Eq/F64Ne/F32Eq/F32Ne but NO ordering ops; `Core::Compare` emit (select.rs:
/// 7805) is integer-only. Only CONSTANT float compares fold (lower.rs:15930).
/// NOT A WASM-OPT SLICE — it is a CORE/NUMERIC gap gated on 2 SEMANTICS decisions the backend must not make
/// unilaterally: (1) EQUALITY `=` is CANONICAL-BYTE (core-semantics.md:313 → NaN=NaN TRUE, -0.0≠+0.0), the
/// OPPOSITE of wasm f64.eq → needs a BITWISE compare + NaN canonicalization, not f64.eq. (2) ORDERING on
/// NaN is spec-UNDEFINED and the const fold DECLINES it (15908/15944); emitting f64.lt (false on NaN) would
/// invent a runtime answer the const path refuses. RE-FILE to the numeric-model/core vertical + design, NOT
/// the wasm backend. Reject-don't-miscompile is currently correct behavior; don't "fix" it with a bare f64
/// compare.
